\section{Background, Threat Model, and Method}
\label{sec:background}

This section establishes the groundwork for the systematization that follows. We first fix the
vocabulary of random number generation and the pipeline that produces it, then state the security
goals a generator must meet together with the adversary models we use throughout the paper, and
finally describe how we assembled and filtered the incident corpus that
Section~\ref{sec:incidents} organizes.

\subsection{Terminology}
We use the vocabulary of the NIST SP 800-90 series, which covers DRBG mechanisms, entropy source
validation, and, since 2025, the construction of complete generators from
both~\cite{nist80090a,nist80090b,nist80090c}, together with RFC 4086~\cite{rfc4086}. \emph{Entropy} is a measure of unpredictability, quantified here in bits
of min-entropy, the negative logarithm of the probability of the most likely value. A
\emph{true random number generator} (TRNG), or entropy source, extracts entropy from a physical
process such as thermal or ring-oscillator jitter. A \emph{pseudorandom number generator}
(PRNG) is a deterministic function that stretches a short seed into a long output. A
\emph{cryptographically secure} PRNG (CSPRNG) is one whose output is computationally
indistinguishable from uniform even to an adversary who sees prior outputs. A \emph{deterministic
random bit generator} (DRBG) is the NIST term for a CSPRNG with explicit instantiate, generate,
and reseed interfaces. A \emph{seed} is the entropy input that instantiates a DRBG, and a
\emph{reseed} injects fresh entropy into a running state. A \emph{nonce} is a value required to
be unique, and sometimes also unpredictable, per use. ECDSA and DSA signatures consume a
secret, unique, unpredictable nonce per signature, and any repetition or bias leaks the signing
key~\cite{bos2014ecc,breitner2019biased}.

A deployed generator is therefore a pipeline. A physical source yields raw noise. An
accumulation and conditioning stage collects, mixes, and health-tests that noise and estimates
its entropy. A DRBG stretches the conditioned seed into output, and the output instantiates keys or
nonces at a cryptographic interface. Conditioning with a hash cannot manufacture entropy:
passing a low-entropy seed through SHA-256 yields output that looks uniform but ranges over no
more values than the seed did~\cite{rfc4086,aumasson2024serious}. Unpredictability is a property of the input
distribution, not of the output's statistics.

\subsection{Security goals and threat model}
Following the robustness literature~\cite{dodis2013devrandom}, a secure generator should provide
\emph{resilience} (output is unpredictable when the adversary influences but does not know the
entropy inputs), \emph{forward security} (a state compromise does not expose past output), and
\emph{backward security} or prediction resistance (a state compromise does not expose future
output once enough fresh entropy is absorbed). Deployed failures correspond to violations of
these goals, and we group the adversaries that cause them into five models used throughout the
paper. The \emph{offline predictor} (T1) knows or can enumerate the low-entropy inputs to
seeding and reconstructs outputs without touching the target, as in Debian OpenSSL, Milk Sad,
and boot-time key generation. The \emph{state-recovery adversary} (T2) learns or
fixes internal state and rolls generation forward, as in DUHK and virtual-machine
reset~\cite{cohney2018duhk,ristenpart2010vmreset}. The \emph{consumption adversary} (T3)
exploits reused or biased nonces and IVs produced at the interface, as in the PlayStation~3 and
cryptocurrency nonce cases~\cite{fail0verflow2010ps3,breitner2019biased}. The \emph{subversion
adversary} (T4) deliberately weakens a standard or product, as in \dualec{}~\cite{shumow2007dualec,bernstein2016dualec}.
The \emph{integration adversary} (T5), which may be an accidental process rather than a
malicious party, causes a build, configuration, or dependency change to substitute a weak path
for a strong one, as in Debian OpenSSL, Juniper, and \coldcard{}.

Orthogonal to what an adversary does is what access it needs, and this dimension turns out to
govern how dangerous a randomness failure is in practice. We distinguish three levels. An
\emph{A1 remote} adversary works only from public data, such as blockchain addresses or TLS
certificates, and never touches the target. An \emph{A2 local} adversary has physical proximity
or code execution on the device and can mount fault-injection or side-channel attacks. An
\emph{A3 supply-chain} adversary influences the artifact before it reaches the victim, whether
through a malicious component, a firmware implant, or an accidental build defect.
Table~\ref{tab:threatmatrix} crosses these levels with the principal attack classes. Failures
exploitable from public data alone (A1) need no per-victim effort, so a single defect can be
harvested across a whole population, as in the Milk Sad, Ill Bloom, and \coldcard{} wallet
thefts~\cite{milksad2023,coinspect2026illbloom,trm2026coldcard}.

