\subsection{Selection method}
\label{sec:selection}
We drew candidate incidents from three source classes: peer-reviewed analyses at security and
cryptography venues, primary vendor advisories and standards documents, and, for the 2026
wallet cases, the vendors' technical postmortems together with independent engineering
analyses~\cite{coinspect2026illbloom,coinkite2026backgrounder,block2026coldcard}. An incident
enters the corpus when three conditions hold: (i) its root cause is a predictable, reused,
biased, leaked, or subverted random value; (ii) the weakness was present in deployed code,
firmware, hardware, or a published standard, whether or not exploitation in the wild was
reported; and (iii) its mechanism is documented well enough to assign a stage. We exclude
laboratory demonstrations on non-deployed targets, failures whose randomness aspect is
incidental (password guessing, brain wallets), and distinguishers with no deployment impact.
Where several reports share one mechanism in one system, we keep the most thoroughly documented
one. The result is twenty-eight incidents from 1996 to 2026 across browsers, TLS and SSH
infrastructure, smart cards, game consoles, mobile platforms, virtualized servers, trusted
platform modules, processors, and cryptocurrency wallets.

The corpus is purposive. We did not run a systematic database search, and we chose incidents to
cover failure mechanisms rather than to estimate how often each occurs. Every count in this
paper therefore describes the corpus, not the population of deployed failures, and is reported
as a count rather than a rate. Incidents that were never disclosed are absent by construction,
which biases the corpus toward failures that were found, usually by exploitation or by
measurement.
